TASK MANAGER APP PLAN (GENERAL APP PLAN WITH MVVM STRUCTURE)

            STEP 1
* Define the app in simple language
    What does the app actually do
        1. Add task
        2. See task
        3. Mark task as done
        4. Delete task

            STEP 2
Break it down into tasks (These are the MVP(Minimal Viable Product))
    1. Task Creation
        * Input field
        * Button to save

    2. Task List
        * Show all task
        * Scrollable list

    3. Task Completion
        * Checkbox / toggle
        * Mark task as done/undone

    4. Task Deletion
        * Remove task from list

            STEP 3
Break tasks into tiny tasks(This is where overwhelm disappears)

FEATURE: Task List
Break into:
    * Create screen layout
    * Add list component
    * Show dummy data

FEATURE: Add Task
    * Create text input
    * Create button
    * Capture text value
    * Add item to list

FEATURE: Completion
    * Add check box UI
    * Toggle state

FEATURE: Delete
    * Add delete button/icon
    * Remove item from list

            STEP 4
Define data
Before coding decide what a task is
Keep it simple
    * Title(String)
    * IsCompleted(Boolean)
Optional later
    * Description
    * Date

            STEP 5
Define screens
Here we need
    * Home Screen
        * Shows list of tasks
        * Has button to add task

    * Add task screren (or dialog)
        * Input field
        * Save button

            STEP 6
Define user flow
Think like a user:
    1. Open app
    2. See tasks
    3. Tap "Add"
    4. Enter task
    5. Save
    6. See it in list
    7. Mark complete or delete

            STEP 7
Define build order
Don't build randomly, build in order
    1. Show empty UI
    2. Show fake tasks
    3. Add new task(in memory)
    4. Toggle completion
    5. Delete task
    6. Save to database

First move (No coding yet)
    * Show list
    * Add task
    * Toggle task
    * Delete task

            TURN THE ABOVE IDEA INTO MVVM BLUEPRINT
Model(data) - What the app stores
viewModel - Manages the logic and state
UI(View) - Displays data and sends users actions
    * UI never talk directly to data
    * Everything flows through viewModel first

        MODEL LAYER(Data)
What data do we have:
    A Task
    Fields:
        * id
        * title
        * isCompleted
This is our core model

* Where does data live?
    * Local database(Room)
    * In memory(List) - For early stages

* Responsibilities of model layer
    * Store tasks
    * Retrieve tasks
    * Update tasks
    * Delete tasks

        VIEW MODEL(Brain of the app)
* What does it hold
    * List of task (state)
    * UI state (loading, empty, etc)

* What actions does viewModel handle
    These comes from the UI
        * addTask(title)
        * toggleTask(Task)
        * deleteTask(Task)
        * loadTask(Later when using DB)

* What viewModel does internally
    * Updates task list
    * Communicates with repository(later)
    * Exposes state to UI

        UI(Compose Screens)
* Screens I need
    * Home screen
        Displays:
            * List of tasks
            * Button to add task
        User Actions:
            * Tap task -> toggle complete
            * Tap delete -> remove task
            * Tap add -> go to add screen
    * Add screen
        Display:
            * Text Field
            * Save button
        User Actions:
            * Enter title -> save task

UI responsibilities
    * Observe task list from viewModel
    * Display tasks
    * Send user actions to viewModel

UI does not(store data, handle business logic)

        DATA FLOW
How everything connects
    * User Action (UI)
    * View Model (handles logic)
    * Repository (Data source later)
    * View Model update state
    * UI recomposes automatically

        REPOSITORY LAYER
Why needed
    * Acts as a single source of truth
    * Connects:
        * Room database
        * API(if added later)

Responsibilities:
    * getTasks()
    * insertTasks()
    * updateTasks()
    * deleteTasks()

                    FINAL PROJECT STRUCTURE
com.yourapp.taskmanager

data/
    model/
        Task.kt
    repository/
        TaskRepository.kt
    local/
        TaskDao.kt
        AppDatabase.kt

ui/
    home/
        HomeScreen.kt
        HomeScreenViewModel.kt(Optional) - But it's cleaner this way (Then remove the viewModel Folder)
    addTask/
        AddTaskScreen.kt
        AddTaskScreenViewModel.kt(Optional) - But it's cleaner this way (Then remove the viewModel Folder)
    components/
        TaskItem.kt
        TaskItemViewModel.kt(Optional) - But it's cleaner this way (Then remove the viewModel Folder)

viewModel/ (Not necessary to stay here, you can move it closer to the UI)
    TaskViewModel.kt

di(optional)

                    BUILD FLOW
Follow this order when coding
    * Step 1 ->
        Show fake tasks
    * Step 2 ->
        Add viewModel (Manage list in memory)
    * Step 3 ->
        Connect UI <-> ViewModel
    * Step 4 ->
        Add interactions
            * Add
            * Toggle
            * Delete
    * Step 5 ->
        Introduce Room + Repository

* Think of the app like this
    * UI - What user sees
    * ViewModel - What app thinks
    * Model - What app remembers

* Remember to move your viewModel closer to your UI(optional)
* Don't over engineer early
    Right now you don't need
        repository/
        local
        di/
    Start with
        data/model/
        ui/
    Then add complexity later

Use only one viewModel, don't split too early, then add more when needed

        THE APP FLOW

        User adds task
        → ViewModel updates _tasks
        → StateFlow emits new list
        → collectAsState receives it
        → Compose recomposes
        → UI shows new task

        GENERAL APP FLOW
        UI (Compose screens)
           ↓
        ViewModel
           ↓
        Repository (optional)
           ↓
        DAO  👈 database access
           ↓
        Room Database